Z||000012||8Ball||08-29-2001||07:06 PM||||I tired the mirror on a stick trick, It still looks like the water is comeing from the shaft itself. So I went ahead and bought a water pump. I figure as old and rusty as it looked I may as well replace it.<P>Ok first off, removeing all the black inner wheel well surroundings (forget what they are called) did not help me a bit  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I could reach everything I can reach now just as easy with them in. But I am in the process of getting all the bolts out and putting them into a traced pattern of the gasket to keep them in order. But the nuts that are on there I think 3 of them. They will not come off the entire bolt is turning with them. Any Ideas?? Also on the water pump I bought The shaft does not screw into it easily, Should I get pliers and screw it in forcefully or should I take it back to advance auto and either get them to put it in or exchange it for another?<P>One more qeustion atm. The pulley wheel. What is the best way to get it off to put it onto my new pump? Is there some way I can lock it into place so it will not spin?<P>Saddly I have this horrible fear that I am going to replace this pump and it will still leak but all the hoses are good as I have replaced several now.<P>Sorry so long but this is my first water pump replacement and I want to make sure I do it right.<BR>Randy||||reg||1
Z||000013||maryjane||08-29-2001||10:59 PM||||I recently (last weekend) changed a waterpump on a Jeep that just POURED water out. When I got it off, the housing itself was eaten away so bad most of it was paper thin and had a big hole near the seal area.<BR>Must have been running with a lot of rust in the system, looked like it had been sandblasted to get it that thin. It was an alum pump. I read the page in the Haynes pos manual on your eng/water pump. Really nothing there that can help you. I have a 4cyl, so I can't help either. Good luck.<BR>Don||||reg||1
Z||000014||8Ball||08-29-2001||11:23 PM||||yea the haynes manual is VERY vauge on it to say the least.I mean they give you str8 4ward instructions on it but I am looking for time savers ect ect||||reg||1
Z||000015||Neal||08-29-2001||11:43 PM||||wow, dont mean to cut you down starfighter but you are going about it in a VERRY difficult way.<P>go to it from the top and top only.<P>move any wires and crap that may be in your way.<P>there are two ways to remove the pulley wheel, (1) use two 13mm wrenches and break bolts against each other you may have to go around like 3 times but it works,  (2) The pump should have 2 holes inside the pulley wheel, a special tool will fit into these and hold the wheel stationary.<P>loosen all bolts, be very carefull not to break the timing cover seal.<P>remove bolts and water pump<P>install new pump by reversing these steps.<P>1.5 hour job||||reg||1
Z||000016||8Ball||08-30-2001||01:20 AM||||does anyone have any ideas about the nuts??<BR>The bolt and all is turning when I try to remove the nuts. I got most the botls out fine but those 3 or 4 nuts are killing me.||||reg||1
Z||000017||87GTZ34||08-30-2001||10:59 AM||||Do you mean nuts holding on wire clips or what.  I don't rememeber any nuts on the backside of the waterpump???  If you can get a digital camera, post a pick that will help alot.<P>I change waterpumps from the top BTW.<BR> <BR>Also, I use the belt wrapped around the pulley and hold the belt together on one side with a vise.  That will clamp the belt tightly around the pulley and hold it.  Also, you can bring the pump/pulley to a shop and have them use an air wrench to take the old bolts out.  Put them back in on the new pump with hand tools though!!! GL||||reg||1
Z||000018||Neal||08-30-2001||11:45 AM||||Im with 87GTZ34 on this one, what nuts are you talking about?  A V6 waterpump should be held in by 12 bolts.  1 of those bolts can potentially have a nut, btu if your spinning the bolt while trying to remove the nut, just leave those for last and bring them out with the pump.||||reg||1
Z||000019||8Ball||08-30-2001||06:54 PM||||Ok guys it is going slowly but I am takeing my time also. I have 3 bolts to go to get the old pump off. 2 are soaking in wd-40 and the last one.... Ahhh yes the last one.<BR>Ok it is at the very very bottom of the water pump and is behind a big pully wheel,<BR>I think this is the crank pully??? I am by no means a mechanic. but anyway I cannot see it or reach it with a tool I can only feel it and I am pretty sure it is a 10 mm can anyone share with me how the heck they got this one off???? pretty please<BR>Randy||||reg||1
Z||000020||wkayl||08-30-2001||07:33 PM||||It's nice to see someone who never has done this kind of a job before diving instead of blasting their car as a piece of *&^%$.  ||||reg||1
Z||000021||Raydar||08-30-2001||09:28 PM||||I don't remember any water pump bolts being behind the crank pulley, but it's been a while since I did mine. <BR>Did you get the bolts out of the water pump pulley? To loosen mine, I just jammed a BIG screwdriver in the back of the pulley, against the bolts, where they stuck through.<BR>Kinda awkward, but it worked.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000022||8Ball||08-30-2001||09:34 PM||||Naa haven't got the wheel off yet but yea there is a bolt at the very bottom. It is onmy new one and the gasket too but I can't reach it for the life of me.<P>As for not calling the car a piece of *&%^<BR>I love Fiero's always have except on the previous one I always paid someone to work on it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
